=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough investigation of every layer along the CS-lab-to-Server-Room path
found no network fault. The CS department hosts (192.168.2.2–.x) reach the
server (pc1, 1.0.0.5/8) via the chain cs_lab → switch1 → router0 →
(wan_r0_r1, 10.10.0.x) → router1 → (wan_r1_r2, 20.20.0.x) → router2 →
switch4 → pc1, with 0 % packet loss and sub-2 ms RTT from every CS device
tested. All three routers are running RIPv2, their routing tables are complete
and fully converged (all eight subnets present, timers fresh), and no
iptables rules exist on any router to filter or drop transit traffic. pc1's
default gateway (1.0.0.1, router2 eth0) is correct and the return path
192.168.2.0/24 is properly learned by router2 via RIP. The RIP configurations
carry no distribute-lists or passive-interface statements that could suppress
routes. The network infrastructure is working exactly as designed.

The user's reported inability to access file shares and the internal server is
not caused by any IP routing, RIP, or firewall problem. The fault must reside
at the application or host level on pc1 itself — most likely a service process
(e.g., SMB/NFS, a web or application server) that has stopped or crashed,
rather than a network outage. The corrective action is to log into pc1
directly and restart the relevant file-sharing and server services; no
changes to the network configuration are required.
=== END ===
